<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Nassau-Usingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1659 CE – 1816 CE · capital Usingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Walramian Nassau principality in the Taunus that moved its court to Biebrich on the Rhine and co-founded the Duchy of Nassau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> was created in 1659 when the lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">Nassau-Saarbrücken</a> were divided among the sons of Count William Louis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walrad-prince-of-nassau-usingen">Walrad</a> received Usingen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a>. Walrad, a general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and imperial service, was raised to prince in 1688.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was Lutheran and mostly rural, but it grew as other Walramian lines died out: Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> inherited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">Nassau-Saarbrücken</a> and Nassau-Idstein in the 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-nassau-usingen">Prince Charles</a> moved the residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biebrich-palace">Biebrich Palace</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>, in 1744, and the spa town of Wiesbaden grew under the princes. After 1797 it lost its left-bank lands but gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> territory in 1803.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usingen">Usingen</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-weilburg">Nassau-Weilburg</a> united as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-duke-of-nassau">Frederick Augustus</a> of Usingen as duke. When he died without sons in 1816, the Usingen line ended and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a> line.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-nassau-usingen.jpg" alt="Principality of Nassau-Usingen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nassau-Usingen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1659 CE – 1816 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Usingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Walrad, Charles, Frederick Augustus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nassau-Usingen</dd></dl></aside></div>
